-# (0230 diagnostic) The allocator "grow" probe: log the grow-call parameters
-# at the abort site (allocator 0x954310, REPORT-LOG108-LOG109.md §7 + the
-# abort diagnosis) so the next device run of an aborting costume pins what
-# overflows. The abort is a deliberate SDK abort (nn::diag::Abort) in the
-# grow branch of 0x954310: a container's inline buffer cannot hold the request
-# (or a count/size overflows), so it calls the heap's grow virtual function V,
-# which aborts. The chain loads a SARC archive (constructor arg "sarc"). V's
-# identity and assertion condition are not pinned by static disassembly, and
-# the discriminating test (log-115: Explorer clothes + Happi cap, still the
-# same abort) proved the trigger is on the BODY/clothes side, not the cap.
-# Two FRAME_STEP cuts in 0x954310, one per grow entry that reaches the virtual
-# call: at 0x954478 (the shared tail: `ldr x8, [x0]`, reached from the
-# bump-overflow entry 0x954428 and the space-check entry 0x954468) and at
-# 0x954390 (the overflow entry 0x954370's own `ldr x8, [x0]`; that entry calls
-# V inline and never reaches the shared tail, so one cut at 0x954478 alone
-# would miss it). Each cut's trampoline passes the grow parameters to one
-# shared body, which appends a 96-byte record to a ring in the frame-step
-# state page at +0x3B20 (immediately after the 0228 headkey ring, which ends
-# at +0x3B20; the state page is 0x4000 bytes, so the ring fits with room to
-# spare); the host drains the ring once per present
-# (dm_diag_grow_frame_tick in hud_manual.c) and logs every record. Grow calls
-# are rare (normal allocations use the inline buffer and never reach the grow
-# path), so the 8-record ring is plenty. Logging-only: the body writes
-# nothing but the ring (never game state), touches no callee-saved register
-# and no flag (every instruction is a load, store, register move/add/sub or a
-# CBZ/CBNZ branch), and executes the replaced instruction before branching
-# back, so the game runs exactly as without the probe. Temporary: remove the
-# cuts and the drain once the abort trigger is pinned.
-# Ring (state +0x3B20): +0x00 u32 magic "GRWR", +0x04 u32 reserved, +0x08 u32
-# write_seq (monotonic, atomic), +0x0C u32 next_off (byte offset of the next
-# slot relative to the record area, 0..0x300, atomic, wraps to 0; the record
-# sits at ring + 0x20 + next_off, so the zero-filled initial value 0 is
-# already the first slot), +0x10..+0x1F reserved, +0x20 records[8] x 96 bytes.
-# Record (96 bytes): +0 u64 container, +8 u64 size, +16 u64 count, +24 u64
-# aligned size, +32 u32 aux, +36 u32 site, +40 u32 seq (written LAST, the
-# commit marker: the host only reads a record whose seq is set), +44 u32
-# reserved, +48 u64 V (the heap grow virtual function pointer, 0234),
-# +56 u64 heap interface pointer ([x25,#0x10], 0234), +64 u64 bump
-# ([container,#0xe0] at the cut, 0234), +72 u64 end ([container,#0xe8] at
-# the cut, 0234), +80..+95 reserved. The aux field is [sp,#0x28] at the cut, which the three grow
-# entries fill differently (overflow: w20 = count; bump-overflow: w8 =
-# [container,#0x90] * count; space-check: w9 = -([container,#0x90] * count)),
-# so aux + count identifies which grow condition fired.
-# Site facts (verified in the disassembly, 0x954310..0x9544d0): at both cuts
-# x21 is the container, x22 the size, w20 the count, x24 the aligned size
-# (align_up(max(size,8),8)), x25 the heap object, x0 the heap interface
-# pointer ([x25,#0x10], read by the replaced instruction), and the stack args
-# are [sp,#8]=container/size, [sp,#0x18]=count, [sp,#0x20]=aligned size,
-# [sp,#0x28]=aux. x0 is the only live caller-saved register at the cuts (x1
-# is rewritten before any read after either cut); x19-x25 and sp are never
-# touched by the body, and the flags are dead at both cuts (the last flag
-# read is the branch that entered the grow path). The replaced instruction
-# is `ldr x8, [x0]` at both cuts (original bytes '080040f9', verified
-# against the NSO).
+# (0240) Route a FrameHeap arena-grow overflow to the global ExpHeap.
+# Root cause (REPORT-0234-PROBE-DATA-ANALYSIS.md, device-probed by the 0234
+# probe in V1.3.62, three runs): the SARC-loading container is a
+# sead::FrameHeap, a fixed-length frame heap (+0xe0 head, +0xe8 tail). Its
+# grow virtual function V (vtable slot 0) aborts by design when called: the
+# abort is not "out of memory", it is "grow called on a heap that cannot
+# grow" (Explorer is short by only 143 KB and still aborts). The allocator
+# 0x954310 serves only this FrameHeap; its three grow entries all call V:
+# the count/size-overflow entry at 0x954370 (inline `blr x8` at 0x954398),
+# the bump-overflow entry at 0x954428 and the space-check entry at 0x954468
+# (both reach the shared tail at 0x954478, `blr x8` at 0x954484). The SARC
+# grow goes through the shared tail (probe site 0x954478, aux 0x1000 =
+# unit*count from the bump-overflow entry), so this fix cuts the shared
+# tail's `blr x8` only; the overflow entry at 0x954398 is a separate V-call
+# site that does not fire for the SARC grow (count 4096 passes the tst
+# check at 0x954368) and is left alone.
+# The cut replaces the `blr x8` at 0x954484 with `b <trampoline>`. The
+# trampoline tries the global heap's allocate (vtable+0x30, the same
+# interface the dispatcher 0x93eb50 uses: `ldr x8,[x1]; ldr x3,[x8,#0x30];
+# br x3`, called as (allocator, size, count)) on the global heap x25, which
+# is the FrameHeap's backing store (an ExpHeap). On success it jumps to
+# 0x95448c, the unlock-if-locked branch (`cbnz x19,#0x95449c`), and the
+# epilogue returns the pointer in x23 -- NOT to 0x954488 (which zeroes x23)
+# and NOT to 0x9544a4 (which skips the unlock). On failure (ExpHeap returns
+# 0) it calls V directly from the trampoline, with the grow request rebuilt
+# (`ldr x0,[x25,#0x10]` for the heap interface, `add x1,sp,#8` for
+# &grow_request); V aborts, exactly as before. It does NOT branch back to
+# 0x954478: the cut at 0x954484 replaced the `blr x8`, so returning there
+# would re-enter the trampoline and loop forever on failure.
+# Register safety: at the cut x25 is the global heap, x22 the size, w20 the
+# count and x19 the lock flag (0, or &mutex at container+0x98), all
+# callee-saved and preserved across the trampoline's `blr` (which clobbers
+# only caller-saved registers and x30; the allocator's x30 is saved on its
+# stack and restored by the epilogue). The trampoline writes only x0/x1/x2/
+# x8 (caller-saved, dead after the V call in the original code) and x23
+# (the return value, set deliberately). sp is never touched, so the grow
+# request at [sp,#8] is intact for the fail path.
+# Cost (intentional, by design): the ExpHeap block is never freed by the
+# FrameHeap reset, so each overflowing SARC load leaks ~1 MB (925,888 /
+# 946,368 bytes); a few MB per game session at most. The SARC data is just
+# bytes, so loading it from the ExpHeap instead of the FrameHeap changes
+# nothing the loader reads.
+# The 0234 grow probe (0230 diagnostic) is REMOVED by this patch: its two
+# cuts (0x954478, 0x954390) and its body are deleted, and its mission is
+# complete (the probe data pinned the root cause). The B cut at 0x97e114
+# (0234 diagnostic) is KEPT: it writes to the same ring (state +0x3B20,
+# kept below for it) and does not conflict with this cut. The 0228 headkey
+# probe and the 0239 head-only fallback are untouched.
+# UNPINNED: the ExpHeap allocate at [x25] vtable+0x30 is inferred from the
+# dispatcher interface (0x93eb50, same `ldr x3,[x8,#0x30]` shape) and has
+# NOT been called on a real device; the mock verifies the logic on host
+# values only. Device acceptance: Explorer / Football / Mechanic enter a
+# kingdom without the abort backtrace, and the good costumes (Happi /
+# Hakama / Armor / Painter) are unaffected (they never reach the overflow
+# path, so this cut is a no-op for them).
+# The ring constants below are kept for the B cut (0234 diagnostic), which
+# writes its records to the same ring at state +0x3B20.
 DIAG_GROW_RING_OFF = 0x3B20
 DIAG_GROW_MAGIC = 0x52575247   # "GRWR"
 DIAG_GROW_REC_SIZE = 96
 DIAG_GROW_REC_CAP = 8
 DIAG_GROW_REC_BYTES = DIAG_GROW_REC_CAP * DIAG_GROW_REC_SIZE   # 0x300: next_off wraps here
 
